1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,360
The next school sections move from mechanisms to patient assessment,
medication safety, monographs, pediatric preparation, scope and
integrated trauma. We will follow that whole decision chain rather
than memorize six disconnected drug cards.

2
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:27,456
First identify immediate threats and a meaningful treatment goal.
Medication must fit within airway, breathing, circulation and
transport priorities, not replace them.

3
00:00:27,456 --> 00:00:40,104
School narrative sometimes contains old or conflicting numbers. In
this lecture, school statements are identified as school statements,
and verified current service rules are identified separately.

4
00:00:40,104 --> 00:00:55,728
Do not guess which contradictory number an instructor intends to
assess. Learn the underlying calculation, record the discrepancy and
use the confirmed school examination version for that assessment,
while never treating it as automatic field authority.

5
00:00:55,728 --> 00:01:08,112
Before treatment, document the problem you are trying to improve and
the patient's current physiological state. Include the drug-specific
safety checks, not just a generic normal-vitals statement.

6
00:01:08,112 --> 00:01:23,520
For breathlessness, the school WHIPS questions ask about the worst
episode, hospital visits, intensive care, puffer requirements and
steroid use. They supplement present assessment rather than deciding
stability by history alone.

7
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:37,416
Check the allergy reaction, all recent doses and relevant
prescription, nonprescription and recreational substances. Duplicate
ingredients in a cold remedy can matter as much as a named
prescription.

8
00:01:37,416 --> 00:01:49,752
Pain scores standardize a patient's report; they do not turn
subjective pain into an objective laboratory result. Pair the score
with function, relevant findings and the person's treatment goal.

9
00:01:49,752 --> 00:02:02,280
Reassess the finding that justified treatment. If bronchospasm was the
goal, compare air entry, effort, speech and symptoms; a new heart rate
alone cannot tell you the treatment worked.

10
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:14,544
Check immediate safety at the same time. After an opioid or sedating
drug, observe depth and quality of breathing and airway protection,
not only a displayed saturation.

11
00:02:14,544 --> 00:02:25,488
Use the current drug-specific reassessment interval and assess sooner
if the patient changes. An interval is not a reason to ignore
deterioration before the next scheduled reading.

12
00:02:25,488 --> 00:02:39,336
Record actual time, dose, route, intended effect and observed
response. A second dose is a new decision requiring the previous
response, cumulative amount and contraindications to be reconsidered.

13
00:02:39,336 --> 00:02:50,472
Drowsiness after medication may be a known effect, but new inability
to protect the airway or inadequate ventilation is not acceptable
simply because sedation was expected.

14
00:02:50,472 --> 00:03:04,584
A sudden pressure fall can follow a vasodilator or rapid injection,
but may also be ongoing bleeding or another deterioration. Support the
patient and reassess cause, rather than assuming one explanation.

15
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:20,088
The school contrasts a morphine-related itch with anaphylaxis.
Isolated itch is different from airway, respiratory or circulatory
compromise, but a suspected non-IgE mechanism cannot rule out a severe
systemic reaction.

16
00:03:20,088 --> 00:03:35,016
Stop a suspected triggering infusion where appropriate, reassess ABCs
and use the anaphylaxis pathway when indicated. Antihistamine symptom
relief must not replace epinephrine for life-threatening anaphylaxis.

17
00:03:35,016 --> 00:03:49,344
An opioid pattern highlights inadequate ventilation and reduced
awareness. Cholinergic excess highlights secretions and bronchospasm.
Identify the immediate physiology before trying to name the exact
chemical.

18
00:03:49,344 --> 00:04:02,856
Sympathomimetic and anticholinergic toxicity can both produce
tachycardia, heat and altered behaviour. Sweating versus dryness can
help, but is not a perfect rule and mixed exposures are possible.

19
00:04:02,856 --> 00:04:16,968
Do not attribute every altered patient to substance use. Hypoxia,
hypoglycemia, stroke, infection and injury can overlap these
presentations. Measure and assess the relevant alternatives.

20
00:04:16,968 --> 00:04:29,904
Protect the team from exposure, request appropriate resources and
provide authorized supportive care. Toxidrome recognition is an
assessment tool, not unrestricted permission to give an antidote.

21
00:04:29,904 --> 00:04:42,240
If the expected effect is absent, first reconsider the assessed
problem. A bronchodilator cannot fix every cause of dyspnea and an
antiemetic cannot correct all causes of vomiting.

22
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:55,200
Check what was actually delivered. Examine the label, amount, timing,
device technique and any vascular site. A calculation on paper does
not prove the dose entered the patient.

23
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:07,896
Consider delayed absorption, poor perfusion, interacting drugs and the
expected onset or peak. Stacking additional medication before those
checks can create a later overshoot.

24
00:05:07,896 --> 00:05:20,856
Maintain immediate supportive care, then decide among an authorized
repeat, a different plan, consultation or escalation. Write the reason
so the next clinician understands the sequence.

25
00:05:20,856 --> 00:05:37,560
Eight A explicitly teaches eight rights: patient, medication, dose,
route, time, reason, documentation and response. Learn that school
list accurately rather than silently replacing it with a differently
numbered mnemonic.

26
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:51,768
BCEHS presents a six-right foundation, and other school material uses
an expanded framework. The count differs because items are grouped
differently; none removes consent, allergy or contraindication checks.

27
00:05:51,768 --> 00:06:04,536
The right patient includes identity and suitability for the
indication. The right response is not something you can guarantee
before giving a drug; it is an obligation to evaluate benefit and harm
afterwards.

28
00:06:04,536 --> 00:06:19,224
A capable patient's refusal still matters even if refusal is not
numbered in this eight-item list. Clarify concerns and apply the
consent and refusal process; do not treat the mnemonic as permission
to override them.

29
00:06:19,224 --> 00:06:32,592
Inspect the actual container at selection, preparation and the final
patient check. Stop for an unclear label, wrong strength, damage,
expiry or an unexpected appearance.

30
00:06:32,592 --> 00:06:46,608
An independent checker uses the original weight, order or monograph
and product label before comparing results. Asking only whether your
stated answer looks right creates an anchoring check, not a truly
independent one.

31
00:06:46,608 --> 00:07:02,112
Label a prepared syringe or infusion with the drug and final
concentration. Keep clean and contaminated handling separate and do
not use a prefilled saline-flush syringe as the container for
medication dilution or storage.

32
00:07:02,112 --> 00:07:15,648
If a discrepancy is discovered, pause that medication, preserve
immediate care and resolve the inputs. Urgency calls for a rehearsed
safe workflow, not abandoning identity and concentration checks.

33
00:07:15,648 --> 00:07:24,384
A useful monograph answers why to give the drug, whether this patient
qualifies, how to prepare it and how to know whether it helped or
harmed.

34
00:07:24,384 --> 00:07:35,232
Read the license-level and clinical-context headings before a dose. A
nearby pediatric or advanced-care table is not automatically part of
PCP authorization.

35
00:07:35,232 --> 00:07:50,952
The school assigns six adult profiles: morphine, methoxyflurane,
acetaminophen, ketorolac, dimenhydrinate and tranexamic acid. The next
slides connect their mechanisms to the most important current safety
distinctions.

36
00:07:50,952 --> 00:08:04,080
The linked sources retain the full current reference. Classroom
examples and mathematical exercises below never substitute for the
complete current clinical screening or an individual authorization
check.

37
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:19,416
Morphine reduces pain through opioid action but can depress breathing
and circulation. The current BCEHS PCP listing is not general
permission for acute-trauma IV morphine; it distinguishes palliative
and interfacility contexts.

38
00:08:19,416 --> 00:08:36,624
The palliative plan requires prior CliniCall or palliative-team
discussion. Its listed adult subcutaneous range is two point five to
five milligrams every ten to thirty minutes, with pressure and plan
conditions and a maximum twenty milligrams per hour.

39
00:08:36,624 --> 00:08:57,720
Interfacility direct dosing has a different maximum and requires CCP A
consultation. Do not carry one context's route or maximum into
another. Read contraindications including respiratory depression,
hypotension, hypersensitivity and head injury with reduced
consciousness, plus the MAO-inhibitor restriction.

40
00:08:57,720 --> 00:09:13,536
Monitor pain, respiratory depth and rate, airway protection, awareness
and pressure. Other depressants increase risk. The school dilution
math can be practised without implying that a student may give the
resulting syringe.

41
00:09:13,536 --> 00:09:29,736
Methoxyflurane is a volatile inhaled analgesic with incompletely
defined molecular action. The patient must understand, cooperate and
self-administer under direct supervision; altered awareness or
instability changes suitability.

42
00:09:29,736 --> 00:09:43,680
Current adult dosing is three millilitres, with a possible repeat
after twenty minutes and a six-millilitre total. Patient limits also
include six millilitres in twenty-four hours and fifteen in a week.

43
00:09:43,680 --> 00:10:04,584
Screen the full exclusions, including under eighteen, pregnancy or
breastfeeding, impaired cooperation or consciousness, renal
impairment, relevant anesthetic-related liver history, tetracyclines,
malignant-hyperthermia history and muscular dystrophy. Monitor
awareness, breathing, pressure and symptoms.

44
00:10:04,584 --> 00:10:19,656
Attach the carbon chamber and use ventilation precautions. Current
provider exposure is at most three doses totaling nine millilitres per
twenty-four hours, not the school's eighteen. Excess exposure can
cause serious renal harm.

45
00:10:19,656 --> 00:10:39,576
Acetaminophen provides analgesia and antipyresis. Screen
hypersensitivity, severe hepatic disease, suspected overdose, last
dose and all-source cumulative intake. Current adult oral dosing is
five hundred to one thousand milligrams, repeatable once after four
hours.

46
00:10:39,576 --> 00:10:54,816
The usual all-source daily maximum is four grams; specified liver
dysfunction or malnutrition reduces it to two grams. Combination
products count. A mathematical remaining allowance does not override a
contraindication.

47
00:10:54,816 --> 00:11:13,704
PCP intravenous use is not a universal fifteen-milligram-per-kilogram
order. The adult table permits one gram for fifty kilograms or more;
the adolescent table has its own age and weight wording. Under-fifty-
kilogram IV dosing is not authorized for PCP in that table.

48
00:11:13,704 --> 00:11:31,584
Eligible one-gram IV doses infuse over fifteen minutes. Monitor for
hypotension; the current monograph states baseline pressure then every
fifteen minutes three times, unlike the school's five-minute summary.
Deterioration demands immediate reassessment.

49
00:11:31,584 --> 00:11:47,424
Ketorolac inhibits COX pathways and can reduce pain, but may cause
renal, gastrointestinal and bleeding harm. Current adult PCP dosing is
ten milligrams IV or IM, repeatable after six hours if indicated.

50
00:11:47,424 --> 00:11:59,520
The IV dose is undiluted over at least fifteen seconds; the IM dose is
direct injection. The school's thirty-milligram adult summary is not
the current BCEHS starting dose.

51
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:18,216
Current contraindications include relevant hypersensitivity, known or
suspected renal dysfunction, concern for severe active bleeding,
pregnancy and anticoagulant use. Severe depletion and recent NSAID use
are listed cautions, not silently renamed absolute exclusions.

52
00:12:18,216 --> 00:12:35,304
For ages two to eleven, the PCP table gives zero point five milligrams
per kilogram IM with a ten-milligram maximum, not the school's
fifteen. The ACP table includes IV; that does not grant the same
pediatric route to PCP.

53
00:12:35,304 --> 00:12:51,288
Dimenhydrinate has H one and anticholinergic effects. Current nausea
indications, frail-patient dosing and age or license headings must be
checked; the school's general antiemetic description is not a dose
order for everyone.

54
00:12:51,288 --> 00:13:05,304
For the school preparation example, one millilitre containing fifty
milligrams plus nine millilitres diluent makes ten millilitres at five
milligrams per millilitre. It does not mean the whole syringe must be
administered.

55
00:13:05,304 --> 00:13:21,432
At that concentration, a twelve-point-five-milligram amount occupies
two point five millilitres. The amount must first be indicated and
authorized. Use the current slow-administration guidance rather than
equating syringe size with dose.

56
00:13:21,432 --> 00:13:35,592
Watch for drowsiness, dry mouth, urinary retention, tachycardia and
local irritation, as well as nausea response. New decreased awareness
can interfere with airway protection and safe assessment.

57
00:13:35,592 --> 00:13:49,488
Tranexamic acid limits fibrinolysis; it does not replace mechanical
bleeding control or directly create a new clot. Eligibility depends on
the actual hemorrhage context, timing and exclusions.

58
00:13:49,488 --> 00:14:03,048
The saved school adult monograph says one gram over ten minutes, while
its integrated case says two grams over one minute. Those are
conflicting regimens, not two descriptions of the same preparation.

59
00:14:03,048 --> 00:14:18,576
The checked current BCEHS adult regimen is two grams IV over one
minute for eligible patients. Apply the complete current eligibility
and exclusion screen and the injury-time restriction; maternal
hemorrhage has a consultation requirement.

60
00:14:18,576 --> 00:14:34,560
For a calculation using one hundred milligrams per millilitre, two
thousand milligrams requires twenty millilitres. That calculation does
not authorize IM, SC or another route, and does not settle an
unconfirmed school exam version.

61
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:46,752
Pediatric handling differs with development, body composition, organ
maturity and illness. A small adult calculation is not enough to
establish safe pediatric treatment.

62
00:14:46,752 --> 00:14:58,512
First verify that this drug and route are authorized at this age and
in this clinical setting. A perfect weight-based calculation cannot
overcome a prohibited route or patient age.

63
00:14:58,512 --> 00:15:08,520
Calculate the amount from the approved weight basis, apply the dose
and cumulative limits, then calculate a measurable volume using the
actual concentration.

64
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:22,992
Include a developmentally appropriate explanation, caregiver
information and baseline physiological assessment. A frightened child
needs calm care, but distress must not obscure respiratory or
circulatory failure.

65
00:15:22,992 --> 00:15:32,784
Establish the dosing weight in kilograms and document whether it was
measured, reported or estimated. Do not make an estimate look like a
scale measurement.

66
00:15:32,784 --> 00:15:46,512
The school emphasizes actual weight or a Broselow-type length tool.
Use the current approved tool within its size range and instructions;
a colour band is an estimate, not universally exact actual weight.

67
00:15:46,512 --> 00:16:00,192
A reported forty-four pounds is about twenty kilograms, not forty-four
kilograms. Keep conversion visible and check whether a drug needs a
particular weight basis rather than assuming one basis fits every
drug.

68
00:16:00,192 --> 00:16:10,152
The checker should verify both the weight input and the computation.
Two people multiplying the same wrong unlabelled number do not prevent
a unit error.

69
00:16:10,152 --> 00:16:21,744
Use a fictional training order of zero point zero eight milligrams per
kilogram for an eighteen-kilogram patient. The kilogram units cancel,
giving one point four four milligrams.

70
00:16:21,744 --> 00:16:34,128
With a product of two milligrams per millilitre, divide one point four
four milligrams by two milligrams per millilitre. Milligrams cancel
and the result is zero point seven two millilitres.

71
00:16:34,128 --> 00:16:45,360
Now check whether the single and cumulative maximum, route,
concentration and measurement precision all allow that result. The
arithmetic is only one part of the decision.

72
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:59,544
Reverse-check: zero point seven two millilitres times two milligrams
per millilitre equals one point four four milligrams. A second
calculation from original inputs should independently reach the same
result.

73
00:16:59,544 --> 00:17:11,616
A fictional protocol says zero point five milligrams per kilogram,
maximum ten milligrams. For twenty-eight kilograms the uncapped
calculation is fourteen milligrams.

74
00:17:11,616 --> 00:17:24,192
Apply the cap before calculating volume: the allowed amount is ten
milligrams if all other criteria are satisfied. At five milligrams per
millilitre that occupies two millilitres.

75
00:17:24,192 --> 00:17:35,784
A maximum is not a target that every smaller patient should receive. A
repeat dose must also respect the cumulative limit and timing, not
merely another single-dose cap.

76
00:17:35,784 --> 00:17:49,440
Do not automatically carry the school's pediatric six-hundred-fifty-
milligram acetaminophen cap into current BCEHS care; its current oral
cumulative and IV eligibility rules differ. Identify the source
version.

77
00:17:49,440 --> 00:18:03,168
The school morphine example starts with ten milligrams in one
millilitre and adds nine millilitres of compatible diluent. The drug
amount stays ten milligrams while the final volume becomes ten
millilitres.

78
00:18:03,168 --> 00:18:14,280
Ten milligrams divided by ten millilitres is one milligram per
millilitre. Adding ten millilitres instead would produce eleven
millilitres and a different concentration.

79
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:27,984
For the school fictional twenty-five-kilogram dose calculation at zero
point zero five milligrams per kilogram, the amount is one point two
five milligrams and the prepared volume is one point two five
millilitres.

80
00:18:27,984 --> 00:18:42,384
This is a calculation demonstration, not a PCP pediatric morphine
order. Label the final concentration, use appropriate measuring
equipment and verify the actual authority before any real
administration.

81
00:18:42,384 --> 00:18:55,248
The school says never round up pediatric doses. Preserve that warning
against casual upward rounding, but recognize that a mathematical
answer may have more decimals than a syringe can measure.

82
00:18:55,248 --> 00:19:07,680
Keep adequate precision during calculations, then use an approved
final-dose or volume-rounding policy and equipment. Do not pretend a
tiny unmeasurable volume was delivered exactly.

83
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:21,960
A standardized compatible dilution may allow safer measurement, but
only if the actual drug, stability, route and preparation procedure
support it. Dilution does not automatically make a wrong drug safe.

84
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:35,496
Use a leading zero for less than one unit and avoid an unnecessary
trailing zero: zero point five, not point five or five point zero when
five is intended. Say the unit every time.

85
00:19:35,496 --> 00:19:50,688
The school calculates a twenty-millilitre-per-kilogram bolus. For
thirty-two kilograms, the arithmetic is six hundred forty millilitres.
That correct multiplication does not prove the whole volume should be
given rapidly.

86
00:19:50,688 --> 00:20:04,368
The checked D zero three pediatric guidance instead considers
increments of five to ten millilitres per kilogram and a twenty-
millilitre-per-kilogram limit. The cause of shock and treatment
response still determine the plan.

87
00:20:04,368 --> 00:20:19,344
BCEHS PCPs may not initiate cannulation under twelve. A fluid
calculation for a younger child is therefore not permission to
establish IV or IO access. Use the authorized care and appropriate
support.

88
00:20:19,344 --> 00:20:34,752
After an authorized increment, compare perfusion, pulse, mental state,
pressure and breathing. New crackles or worsening effort may signal
harm. Do not give repeated fluid simply to normalize every pressure
number.

89
00:20:34,752 --> 00:20:46,608
The regulation distinguishes category services from services requiring
an endorsement. A possible Schedule Two service is not proof that
every PCP currently holds that endorsement.

90
00:20:46,608 --> 00:21:01,752
The practitioner must also meet individual restrictions, training and
competence requirements, and the employer must operationalize the
intervention. An employer guideline cannot by itself create a legal
permission that does not otherwise exist.

91
00:21:01,752 --> 00:21:16,728
The school says employers may expand scope. Read that as
implementation within valid legal authority, not permission to exceed
the law. Medical consultation also does not automatically remove a
legal restriction.

92
00:21:16,728 --> 00:21:29,232
Finally, a permitted intervention still needs an appropriate patient
indication, valid consent or other authority and an acceptable risk-
benefit judgment. May do does not mean must do.

93
00:21:29,232 --> 00:21:44,376
The school calls tracheal intubation standard for PCPs in one passage.
The Board's January twenty twenty-five statement says recognized PCP
programs do not include it and BCEHS does not operationalize it at PCP
level.

94
00:21:44,376 --> 00:21:56,760
A listed possible endorsement and an actual recognized pathway are
different. Do not infer that passing an entry-level exam grants the
intervention or the sedation medications needed for it.

95
00:21:56,760 --> 00:22:11,400
For ipratropium, the current public monograph names PCP severe
bronchospasm and pediatric consultation. It does not independently
verify the school's universal claim about an ePCR-profile endorsement
display.

96
00:22:11,400 --> 00:22:23,784
Verify the actual licence, required education and employer status
rather than inventing their contents. Keep a school claim marked
unconfirmed if the complete policy is not accessible.

97
00:22:23,784 --> 00:22:39,720
Current PCP intranasal ketamine requires the specified flight-training
and endorsement pathway plus prior consultation. The monograph
excludes age under five, rather than merely requiring a consult below
two as the school matrix says.

98
00:22:39,720 --> 00:22:52,608
Current glucagon listings include FR and EMR hypoglycemia contexts
with age conditions. That clinical listing still does not prove an
individual has completed required training or endorsements.

99
00:22:52,608 --> 00:23:05,592
Hydromorphone PCP use is linked to palliative or end-of-life pain and
a prior CliniCall or palliative-team dosing plan. Do not convert this
into general permission for acute-trauma opioid treatment.

100
00:23:05,592 --> 00:23:22,152
The school describes a licensing-exam nitroglycerin update. The
checked current BCEHS EMR and PCP monograph still lists heart rate
below fifty or above one hundred fifty. Distinguish the versions and
verify every other contraindication too.

101
00:23:22,152 --> 00:23:35,880
The school abbreviates PCP IO to a tibial site, two attempts and
hypovolemic arrest. Current PR twelve also specifies training and the
age-twelve-or-older condition for the independent pathway.

102
00:23:35,880 --> 00:23:48,096
The reference includes an on-scene ACP-or-higher supervision
alternative. Do not omit that alternative, but do not use it to erase
the stated prohibition on PCP cannulation under twelve.

103
00:23:48,096 --> 00:23:59,688
The tibia is the approved PCP site, and attempts are two collectively
per patient, not two per limb or per provider. Do not recannulate a
failed or dislodged site.

104
00:23:59,688 --> 00:24:12,408
IO attempts must not detract from effective CPR, airway management or
defibrillation. These scope distinctions are preparation for
supervised skills, not a claim of competence from watching slides.

105
00:24:12,408 --> 00:24:28,440
A fifty-six-year-old worker is injured by a moving industrial panel.
There is major forearm bleeding, pallor and confusion. The equipment
has not yet been isolated. This is an original teaching case, not the
school's assessed case.

106
00:24:28,440 --> 00:24:40,800
Observe the mechanical hazard and arrange safe access before contact.
Delegate bleeding control with the indicated trained method while the
team assesses airway, breathing and perfusion.

107
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:53,112
Prevent heat loss, arrange urgent appropriate transport and request
additional help. Do not delay these actions while one person reads
every drug card or attempts an unnecessary line.

108
00:24:53,112 --> 00:25:07,992
A medication plan now needs actual vital signs, injury timing,
contraindications and individual authority. Pain does not
automatically make morphine appropriate, and giving TXA does not prove
bleeding is controlled.

109
00:25:07,992 --> 00:25:22,608
Assume the instructor confirms adult TXA eligibility and the current
two-gram IV plan for this practice. The product is one hundred
milligrams per millilitre. State two grams equals two thousand
milligrams.

110
00:25:22,608 --> 00:25:36,720
Divide two thousand milligrams by one hundred milligrams per
millilitre to get twenty millilitres. An independent checker verifies
the patient, regimen, container and calculation before the preparation
is connected.

111
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:51,480
The school also practises five-hundred-millilitre adult fluid
increments and a limited-pressure trauma strategy. Do not turn that
into a universal target: head, spinal and other shock contexts may
require different plans.

112
00:25:51,480 --> 00:26:03,984
State the treatment goal and reassess after each authorized increment.
A palpable radial pulse is not a measured pressure and does not by
itself prove adequate organ perfusion.

113
00:26:03,984 --> 00:26:17,040
During movement the patient becomes quieter, breaths become shallower
and pressure falls. Stop treating the first written plan as fixed.
Return to immediate assessment and support failed functions.

114
00:26:17,040 --> 00:26:28,608
Recheck hemorrhage control and the vascular site, look for hidden
injury and confirm what medications and volumes were actually
administered. One deterioration can have more than one cause.

115
00:26:28,608 --> 00:26:43,224
If a sedating medicine was given, assess its contribution without
assuming it explains every finding. A new rash with airway or
circulatory compromise needs the relevant reaction pathway, not just a
label of side effect.

116
00:26:43,224 --> 00:26:55,536
Communicate the change, request appropriate help and revise the
transport and treatment plan. Record before-and-after data so the
receiving clinician can follow what changed and when.

117
00:26:55,536 --> 00:27:07,368
Transfer the indication and relevant allergy or risk information, then
every actual dose and its time. Saying that pain medicine was given is
not enough to prevent duplication.

118
00:27:07,368 --> 00:27:19,488
Use both drug amount and relevant concentration or volume when needed
for verification, with units. Clearly identify an infusion that is
still running and its rate and remaining contents.

119
00:27:19,488 --> 00:27:32,136
State the response with measured times, including any adverse effect
and action taken. An uncompleted medication or prepared-but-not-given
syringe must not be reported as administered.

120
00:27:32,136 --> 00:27:45,864
The receiving clinician needs cumulative intake, last dose, unresolved
symptoms and the next restriction, not just a reassuring final
snapshot. Confirm that responsibility has actually transferred.

121
00:27:45,864 --> 00:27:58,464
Without notes, explain why dose accuracy, clinical suitability and
authorization are three different requirements. Give an example in
which the arithmetic is right but the treatment is not permitted.

122
00:27:58,464 --> 00:28:09,336
Complete the original calculation exercises and reverse-check the
units. Identify which school dose statements conflict internally or
with the checked current service source.

123
00:28:09,336 --> 00:28:24,000
In a supervised rehearsal, speak the medication check, label the
preparation and ask the checker to use original inputs. Then say the
expected effect, adverse warnings and reassessment plan before giving
anything.

124
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:35,880
